First Author: Kainuma
Title: Predictors of 1-year outcome after cardiac re-transplantation: Machine learning analysis
Source number: 23

Very short summary: This retrospective UNOS registry study used random survival forests to identify predictors of 1-year mortality after cardiac re-transplantation. Five key findings were: (1) 1294 adult cardiac re-transplant recipients were analyzed; (2) 137 underwent early re-transplantation (≤1 year after first transplant) and 1157 underwent late re-transplantation (>1 year); (3) 1-year mortality was markedly higher after early versus late re-transplantation, 40.6% versus 13.6%; (4) in the early group, total bilirubin >2 mg/dL was the top RSF predictor of 1-year mortality with VIMP 2.99%; and (5) in the late group, ventilator dependence and BMI >30 kg/m² were the leading RSF predictors, with VIMP 1.47% and 1.43%, respectively. Figures 1–4 and Tables 1–3 were used directly for this extraction.

