Section 6: Quality Assessment (Risk of Bias / PROBAST)
	•	Participants (Selection Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The cohort came from the national UNOS registry and included all adult cardiac re-transplant recipients meeting criteria between 2000 and 2020, which reduces single-center selection bias.
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Predictors were clinically relevant recipient and donor factors measured at transplant, including end-organ function, support devices, hemodynamics, donor risk, and ischemic time.  ￼
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The modeled outcome was clearly defined as time to 1-year mortality, analyzed with Kaplan–Meier, Cox modeling, and RSF variable importance for 1-year death.
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): Unclear | Quote/Reasoning: Strengths include national scope and complementary RSF plus Cox analyses, but no held-out validation metrics, calibration assessment, or external validation were reported in the retrieved text.
	•	Applicability to Review Question: Low Concern | Quote/Reasoning: This article directly addresses machine-learning-based risk stratification in heart transplantation, specifically cardiac re-transplantation, and is closely aligned with the review question.

